                acceptedAnswer: {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  text:
                    "Yes. Endoscopic lumbar discectomy is an effective option for recurrent disc herniation, allowing targeted removal of recurrent fragments while minimizing scar tissue disruption and preserving stabilizing structures compared with repeat open surgery."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                name:
                  "What is the expected recovery timeline after endoscopic cervical or thoracic surgery?",
                acceptedAnswer: {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  text:
                    "Most patients are able to ambulate on the day of surgery or the next day, return to light daily activities within several days, and resume normal function within a few weeks, depending on the level treated, underlying pathology, and overall health status."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                name:
                  "Are there patients who should not undergo endoscopic spine surgery?",
                acceptedAnswer: {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  text:
                    "Contraindications include marked spinal instability, significant deformity requiring realignment, spinal infection, tumors requiring en bloc resection, or situations where endoscopy cannot provide adequate decompression or stabilization."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                name:
                  "How does endoscopic spine surgery compare to fusion surgery in terms of motion preservation?",
                acceptedAnswer: {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  text:
                    "Endoscopic spine surgery focuses on removing the offending pathology while preserving native motion segments, avoiding the stiffness and adjacent segment degeneration associated with fusion procedures in appropriately selected patients."
                }
              }
            ]
