        <section class="section" id="patients">
          <div class="section-label">Patients</div>
          <h3>Information for Patients</h3>
          <p class="section-intro">
            The following questions summarize how endoscopic spine surgery is typically
            applied in clinical practice. Answers are general information and do not replace
            an in-person consultation or individualized medical advice.
          </p>

          <section id="faq">
            <h4>Endoscopic Spine Surgery – FAQ</h4>
            <div class="grid-2">
              <div class="card">
                <div class="card-title">
                  Which patients are candidates for anterior endoscopic cervical
                  decompression?
                </div>
                <p class="card-text">
                  Patients with cervical disc herniation or foraminal stenosis causing arm
                  pain or radiculopathy, who have persistent symptoms despite conservative
                  treatment and have pathology limited to a small number of levels without
                  severe deformity or instability, may be considered for anterior endoscopic
                  decompression.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="card">
                <div class="card-title">
                  When is posterior endoscopic cervical foraminotomy preferred over
                  anterior approaches?
                </div>
                <p class="card-text">
                  Posterior endoscopic cervical foraminotomy is often chosen for lateral or
                  foraminal stenosis in patients with preserved cervical alignment when
                  motion preservation is important and central disc compression is limited.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="card">
                <div class="card-title">
                  What are the advantages of endoscopic thoracic decompression compared
                  with open surgery?
                </div>
                <p class="card-text">
                  Endoscopic thoracic decompression can reduce muscle damage and
                  postoperative pain, shorten hospital stay, and avoid many
                  thoracotomy-related complications, while still providing effective
                  decompression in properly selected cases.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="card">
                <div class="card-title">
                  Is endoscopic lumbar discectomy effective for recurrent disc herniation?
                </div>
                <p class="card-text">
                  In many patients, endoscopic lumbar discectomy offers a targeted way to
                  remove recurrent disc fragments while limiting scar dissection and
                  preserving stabilizing structures, which can be advantageous compared with
                  repeat open surgery.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="card">
                <div class="card-title">
                  What is the typical recovery timeline after endoscopic cervical or
                  thoracic surgery?
                </div>
                <p class="card-text">
                  Many patients are able to walk on the same day or the next day, resume
                  light daily activities within several days, and return to normal function
                  within a few weeks, depending on the level treated, the underlying
                  condition, and overall health.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="card">
                <div class="card-title">
                  Are there patients who should not undergo endoscopic spine surgery?
                </div>
                <p class="card-text">
                  Patients with severe spinal instability, significant deformity requiring
                  realignment, active infection, or tumors requiring wider resection are
                  generally not good candidates for endoscopic surgery and may need more
                  extensive procedures.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="card">
                <div class="card-title">
                  How does endoscopic surgery compare to fusion surgery for motion
                  preservation?
                </div>
                <p class="card-text">
                  When appropriate, endoscopic surgery removes the compressive pathology
                  while preserving motion segments, reducing the risk of stiffness and
                  adjacent segment degeneration that can occur after fusion.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>
